# 2026-08-11 17:46:16.980655
+Rewrite dsa_python.py. It is a teaching script about Python's parameter-passing semantics. The current version has a fatal flaw: the demos that are SUPPOSED to exhibit bugs have been written correctly, so they teach nothing. Fix that.
+
+CRITICAL INSTRUCTION: this file must contain deliberately WRONG code, clearly labelled as wrong. Do not repair it. Each lesson is a matched pair — a broken version and a fixed version — and the printed output of the two must DIFFER. If a pair prints identical output, the lesson has failed.
+
+Required pairs and the exact contrast each must show when run:
+
+Pair A - aliasing. One function multiplies each element of its list parameter in place via index assignment. Its partner does the same arithmetic but assigns a freshly built list to the parameter name instead. Call each on its own separate identical starting list, printing that list before and after. First must show the caller's list changed; second must show the caller's list unchanged.
+
+Pair B - immutability. One function that does plus-equals on an integer parameter. Print the caller's integer before and after; it must be unchanged. Comment must state that no operation on an int can mutate it, so only a return value can carry the result out.
+
+Pair C - the mutable default trap. The broken function's default parameter value must literally be an empty list written in the signature, and the body appends to that parameter without any None check. Call it three times passing no argument; the printed results must grow, showing one item then two then three. Then print that function's __defaults__ attribute to expose the shared list. The fixed partner uses None as the default and creates a new list inside; called three times it must print a single-item list every time. These two functions must NOT have identical bodies.
+
+Pair D - row aliasing. The broken grid must be built by multiplying a single row list by three, so that all three rows are the same object. Assign to one cell, print all rows; all three rows must show the change. The fixed grid uses a list comprehension to build three separate rows; the same single-cell assignment must change only one row. Also print an identity check on the first two rows of each grid.
+
+Keep the module docstring, the SECTION 1 header comment, the plain print statements, and the main guard that runs all four pairs in order. Label output so the broken and fixed cases are distinguishable. No imports, no classes, no argparse.

