# 2026-08-11 17:54:57.707811
+Create a new file containing teaching demos for Python's built-in any and all. Write ONLY function definitions plus one section header comment reading 'SECTION 2: any() AND all()'. Do NOT write a module docstring and do NOT write an if __name__ main guard; this file will later be spliced into a larger script.
+
+Define exactly six zero-argument demo functions, each printing its own clearly labelled output, each preceded by a comment of one or two lines stating the lesson.
+
+Demo 1, password rules. Hold the rules in a dictionary mapping a short human readable rule name to a small predicate over a candidate string. Rules: minimum length of eight, contains a digit, contains both an uppercase and a lowercase letter, contains a character that is not alphanumeric. Note that the inner rules are themselves naturally expressed with any over the characters, so this demo nests any inside all. Provide a validity check using all across the rules, and separately a list of the names of the rules that failed, to make the point that all yields only a verdict and discards which check failed. Exercise it on one password that fails several rules and one that passes.
+
+Demo 2, service health. A dictionary of service names to integer status codes with one service not equal to two hundred. Print whether every service is healthy using all, whether any service is down using any, and the list of unhealthy service names.
+
+Demo 3, log scanning. A tuple of alert substrings and a few log lines of which one contains an alert word. For each line use any over the substrings to decide whether to report it. Make the comment say that keeping the patterns as data rather than a chain of or conditions is the real benefit.
+
+Demo 4, empty iterables. Print the result of any and of all applied to an empty list. The comment must name this vacuous truth and warn that an all over an empty collection is true, which silently approves empty input.
+
+Demo 5, short circuiting. A helper predicate that prints each value as it is examined and reports whether it exceeds two. Drive it with any over a generator expression across the integers one through five, so the printed trace proves evaluation stops early rather than examining all five.
+
+Demo 6, generator versus list. Using the same printing helper, contrast passing a generator expression to any against passing a bracketed list comprehension, printing a header before each so the reader can compare the two traces and see that the brackets destroy the early exit.
+
+No imports of any kind. No classes. No argparse. Use plain print statements throughout.

