# 2026-08-11 17:58:42.332321
+Create a new file of teaching demos about object identity and hashing. Write ONLY a section header comment reading 'SECTION 3: id() AND hash()' followed by function definitions. No module docstring, no main guard, no imports of any kind, no argparse. This file will be spliced into a larger script later.
+
+Define exactly seven zero-argument functions whose names all start with the demo_ prefix. Each prints clearly labelled output and is preceded by a one or two line comment stating the lesson.
+
+Demo 1, identity versus equality. Two separately built lists holding the same values. Show that comparing them with the equality operator is true while comparing them with the identity operator is false, then bind a third name to one of them and show that this alias is identical to it. The comment must say that equality asks about value and identity asks about which object.
+
+Demo 2, address recycling. Call the identity builtin on a freshly constructed throwaway object several times in a loop, where nothing keeps the object alive, and print each result so the reader sees the same number reappear. Then repeat, but this time append each object to a list so they all stay alive, and print those results so the reader sees every number differ. The comment must state that an identity number is only guaranteed unique among objects that are alive at the same moment, and that once an object is discarded its address can be handed to the next object.
+
+Demo 3, string interning. Compare with the identity operator two separately written string literals that look like a Python identifier, and then two separately written string literals that contain dot characters. Also build a string at runtime by joining pieces and compare it by identity and by equality to an equal literal. The comment must explain that the interpreter deduplicates certain compile-time strings as an optimisation, that this is not something a program may rely on, and that equality is the only correct test.
+
+Demo 4, the hash contract. Show that the integer one, the float one, and the boolean true are all equal to one another and all produce the same hash. Then show the consequence: a dictionary created with the integer as key can be read with the float as key, and a set built from all three collapses to a single element. The comment must state the rule that objects which compare equal are required to hash equal.
+
+Demo 5, hashable versus unhashable. Attempt to hash each of several objects covering both mutable and immutable builtin container types plus a string, catching the failure for the ones that refuse and printing which succeeded and which failed with the reason. Then attempt to hash a tuple that contains a list, to show that a container of mutable contents is itself unhashable. The comment must state that hashability requires immutability because a hash must not change during an object's lifetime.
+
+Demo 6, hash stability. Print the hash of a fixed string several times within this run to show it does not change, and print the hash of a few small integers to show that for integers the hash is simply the integer value. The comment must warn that string hashes are deliberately salted with a random seed chosen at interpreter start, so they differ between separate runs of the program even though they are stable within one run, and that hashes must therefore never be persisted to a file or database.
+
+Demo 7, mutating a key. Define a tiny class whose hash and equality are both derived from a single attribute. Put one instance into a dictionary as a key and confirm the value can be retrieved. Then reassign that attribute on the live key object, and show that the entry can no longer be found by a lookup matching either the old attribute value or the new one, even though printing the dictionary contents proves the entry is still physically present. The comment must connect this to mutable parameters: a mutation reaching a shared object can silently corrupt a data structure built on top of it.

