# 2026-08-11 18:04:22.783778
+Correct one function in section3.py, demo_string_interning. Reproduce every other function unchanged.
+
+The function is factually wrong. It compares two dotted string literals written next to each other and its trailing comment claims the identity test yields false, but it actually yields true, so the file teaches the opposite of reality. The underlying rule is subtler than the function currently implies and must be shown properly.
+
+The real rule has two independent parts and the rewritten function must separate them into two clearly headed groups of output.
+
+First group, within a single compilation unit. When one chunk of source is compiled in one go, the compiler collapses duplicate equal string constants into a single shared object. Demonstrate this using two side by side literals containing dots, and show that the identity test between them is true. State in the printed label that this sharing happens for any equal constants in the same unit, whatever characters they contain, and is a compiler optimisation rather than interning.
+
+Second group, across separate compilation units. This is what the interactive prompt does, since it compiles each entered line on its own. Demonstrate it without importing anything by using the two builtins that turn source text into a code object and then execute a code object against a supplied namespace dictionary. Compile two separate one line assignment sources that each bind the same dotted literal, execute each into its own fresh dictionary, and compare the two resulting strings by identity, which is false. Then repeat that whole procedure with a literal whose characters are all letters so that it looks like a Python identifier, and show the identity test is now true.
+
+Add explanatory comment lines stating the conclusion: strings that look like identifiers are automatically interned so equal ones are shared everywhere in the process, strings containing characters such as dots are not, and therefore two equal strings typed on separate interactive lines may or may not be the same object. End with the practical rule that identity must never be used to compare string values and equality is the only correct test. Every trailing comment that predicts an output value must match what the code actually prints.

